model=qwen2.5:3b
Gravity is a natural force that exists between all objects with mass or energy in the universe. It's what keeps our feet on the ground and causes planets to orbit stars, as well as satellites to orbit around Earth. On Earth, we experience gravity as the downward pull of an object towards another object. Gravity is one of the four fundamental forces of physics (the others being electromagnetism, strong nuclear force, and weak nuclear force), and it acts at a distance, meaning that it affects objects even when they are not in direct contact with each other.

According to Einstein's theory of general relativity, gravity is caused by the curvature of spacetime created by mass and energy. Objects in the universe don't just "fall" towards each other; rather, their paths or trajectories are bent due to this curvature of space-time, which we perceive as gravitational force.